# Native Nyström spectrum audit: interpretation

The repaired producer task completed under the 4 GiB CPU guard. Its independent
raw-table audit passed every inventory, identity, spectrum-order and summary
check. The first attempt remains failed because author `samplers.py` imported
unavailable `h5py` for unrelated loaders; the accepted v2 used a pinned fail-closed
stub and never called HDF5 functionality.

Across the frozen native correlated-Gaussian sample there were 448 rows. The
released `1e-12` singular-value floor was crossed in 135 rows (30.13%). It was
never crossed for k=14,28,42,56,70; it was crossed for 23/56 k=140 rows and every
k=350 and k=500 row. The k=350 rows had 5,145 singular values below the floor in
aggregate, and k=500 rows had 11,231. The smallest observed singular value was
`2.07e-20`, or `2.07e-8` times the floor. SVD versus symmetric-eigenvalue spectra
agreed to a maximum relative discrepancy of `1.62e-15`, supporting the measurement.

This converts the w007 concern from a deliberately synthetic boundary into an
observed numerical condition within a finite sample of the released native data
generator and default landmark grid. It does not show that decisions, null size,
or power are wrong, and the fixed reproducibility seeds are not the paper's saved
400/1000-trial seeds. The next discriminating step is an outcome-labeled selected-
case comparison of the released floor with explicit absolute and relative spectral
truncation policies, including the exact permutation-score order and direct native
decision. No mapping is called a correction before its mathematical target and
statistical behavior are established.

Peak producer RSS was 136,339,456 bytes; the verifier used 18,350,080 bytes. The
task was not memory-blocked.
